mr shaps we're told that the omicron is  everywhere yet all of a sudden we're  seeing a lot of these rules being  removed are we not letting down our  defenses a little bit too early  no definitely not uh this was always  brought in on a temporary basis and it  did its job we since bringing in the the  red list and then these additional tests  managed to get 13 million extra jabs the  booster jab in people's arms so it  served a purpose obviously omicron is  global it's worldwide everybody has it  now the proportion that comes in from  overseas is completely uh is minuscule  by comparison with the numbers uh that  are actually here so um it's now  widespread and uh in a sense omicron  testing has outlived its usefulness so  we're able to withdraw it from uh  international uh travel uh today uh  domestically of course and carry on  sorry  but that's omicron what about other  variants  by doing this we are potentially adding  loads of other variants in without  testing people before they come to the  uk  well because it's global um we're just  as likely to perhaps have a variant here  we saw that last year with the year  before now with the kent variant also  called alpha  so of course a variant can can occur  anywhere this country does do more  genomic sequencing that's when you find  out that what the variant might be than  any other country in the world so we're  still carrying on that massive  sequencing program we'll carry on doing  that because if you get a positive  lateral flow test after you've traveled  and that is the one test that remains  you must take a lateral flow test by day  two um and if you do get a positive then  that needs to be  pcr'd on the nhs and sequenced so we've  still got that protection in place but  we think this is proportionate a lot of  jobs depend on travel a lot of jobs  depend on tourism and we need to keep  these uh measures in line  with actually what's going on with  omicron around the world which is is  everywhere now it's the predominant uh  strain of coronavirus  not everyone's convinced although the  welsh government health minister said  that she was making the changes  reluctantly uh and the stress that the  welsh government was still advising  against all but essential international  travel why do you think they're not  concerned  yeah look it's always been for the  different devolved administrations  around the uk to decide as far as i'm  aware actually the whole of the uk is  going to be moving on this um together  so these rules will apply um everywhere  and these are judgment calls it's  absolutely uh right to say but but i do  have to say the idea that somehow the  united kingdom is is safe and secure but  if you travel outside of our borders uh  then you know somehow it will be you  know it's a dangerous world out there um  i think sort of belies the truth which  is that omicron is everywhere around the  world we've got very significant testing  in place and i don't think any country  does more testing  than we do per head of population so for  example on tuesday this week  i saw there were over 2 million  coronavirus tests which took place um  and so we you know we keep a very very  close eye on these things but you know  uh people still need to be able to get  around and see a family perhaps  elsewhere in the road in the world  business needs to be done elsewhere and  as i say  many uh we we have pretty high levels of  coronavirus um here so it's not like if  you go abroad you're necessarily going  somewhere with higher levels you may  well be somewhere with lower levels so  we just didn't see the reason to keep  what we're always temporary measures in  place for a moment longer  than was useful and in a sense they just  outlived their usefulness when it comes  to international travel  do we have a shortage of tests  no we don't uh when we get another  spokesman yesterday said that changes  were to make the best use of our testing  system at a time of high prevalence  slightly suggests that that  there's a certain amount of of  prioritizing yeah no that was  specifically in reference to uh not  requiring to do a pcr test if you're  asymptomatic and you take a lateral flow  you no longer need to confirm that with  a  pcr test so that was in reference  something slightly different and yes it  is true to say that we want to make sure  that we're focusing those tests on  critical workers on the front line uh  as for example with transport workers or  with health workers to make sure they're  available so we don't want to over test  for no reason but remember the travel  tests were always provided by the  private sector and that single lateral  flow that's still required will still be  a private sector uh test um but we've  got a huge amount of testing um  available as i mentioned we tested two  million in a single day on tuesday  and  and there are tests available for people  who need to get pcr tests uh lateral  flow supply uh is is very much available  sometimes delivery of those tests is  proving uh challenging and we're  increasing the number of delivery routes  available through the royal mail and  elsewhere as well  could you just clarify the rules on  testing for us and the advice seems to  be that if you have one of the three  main symptoms  that you should get a pcr otherwise not  now this is confusing quite a lot of  people because a lot of people with  omicron have aches and pains and not  necessarily a temperature and a cough  should they just do a lateral flow test  yeah that's right so um just to clarify  this if you have one of the main  symptoms that they're listed on the nhs  website um then by all means booker a  pcr test for other people who are  perhaps going about their normal lives  but maybe feel they'll be in a position  where they'll meet vulnerable people  that's where the lateral flow tests are  available i know a lot of people over  christmas myself included taking lateral  flow tests before going to see my  elderly parents and that's good practice  and it helps to protect everyone in fact  in my family  my my eldest son took a test had  absolutely no symptoms whatsoever and  found he was positive and then had to go  and self-isolate for for nine days until  he got uh two days of negative uh  lateral flow tests so that's the purpose  of lateral flow tests particularly good  where people are asymptomatic and it can  find people who who've got cases but pcr  tests where you are symptomatic that's  that's the message  and it seems to me that the current  message from the government generally  seems to be that we are just going to  have to live with covert as you were  saying you know  it's not the case that there's a big bad  world out there we have a lot of it in  the country um but there's a a lot of of  messages coming from the nhs from staff  and we have this report out today about  pressure on the nhs  and they feel that actually living with  it  is just not possible that we are  reaching a crisis point in the in the  nhs what do you say to that  we've always known in dealing with  omnichrome and this is where our plan b  has been in place that we are uh if you  like always trying to find the right  compromise between going too tight on  restrictions lockdowns and let's face it  they have a lot of costs uh connected  and monetary costs obviously to the  economy but also to people's jobs also  for people's mental health  of these lockdowns  and then again not wanting  our hospitals to be overrun and this is  where i think the plan b has been shown  to be the right approach so far  we we know that uh that  there are hospitals with critical  warnings at the moment that that doesn't  seem to be like it's going swimmingly  for them  yeah so uh there are 137 uh trusts there  are 24 which are uh critical uh it's not  entirely unusual for hospitals to go  critical over the winter often with  things like the flu pandemic but there  are very real pressures which i  absolutely  recognize and we know what it's like  when these hospitals are under pressure  my dad was in hospital this time last  year when there were 40 000 people in  hospital with coronavirus and the  pressures were very real this year we've  got 5 000 more doctors  10 000 more nurses in place uh and it's  very important that we do provide all  those uh resources and and we've we've  we've provided an extra 5.9 billion  pounds to hospitals a 36 billion pound  catch-up program uh as well  but i do absolutely recognize those  pressures as i say what we want to do is  steer the country through the omrichrom  uh wave and i think so far plan b is  shown to have been the right way forward  not shutting down the whole country yet  again with all the costs of people's  mental health uh and elsewhere uh but  making sure that we're providing the  extra resources uh to hospitals uh  unfortunately in terms of those extra  resources  in terms of those extra resources a  report out from mps today uh saying that  nhs waiting lists which are already huge  are to double by 2025  huge numbers uh in the northeast  especially  what is your solution to that  yeah i mentioned the amount of money  that we're putting into tackling those  waiting lists so we've got a 5.9 billion  catch up for 36 million that i that i  mentioned um it's one of the reasons why  we took what most people would accept  was a difficult decision but nonetheless  i think worthwhile one uh last year  which is to say that we will  be having that uh national insurance  rise in order to pay uh in part for  catch up on the number of operations and  routine procedures which because  we've now been tackling coronavirus for  what is it nearly two years uh have by  necessity in some cases not been able to  to take place we want to make sure that  we catch up with that we're putting  record sums into  our nhs we love the nhs we want it to be  able to function i think most people  watching a program accept that of course  these have been exceptional times we've  never faced anything  like a virus like this before in modern  history yeah and in terms of support in  the cabinet jacob rees-mogg not quite as  in favor of the national insurance rise  uh as others we understand from  yesterday's cabinet meeting how did did  his suggestion that that should be  scrapped the rise go down well  cabinets take place in in in private was  i'm not going to get into commenting on  uh cabinet meeting uh content uh we have  a plan uh it is to catch up on uh on  things like the backlog in uh in nhs uh  care which has come about uh  unfortunately through coronavirus and  that plan is being put into into place  uh you know it is also true to say that  we want to make sure that we relieve tax  pressures uh on public uh as and when we  can but you know that has to be done  line government could change their mind  on this and scrap it  oh well look we're absolutely committed  to doing two things one catching up with  the backlog in the nhs and that's why we  have this national insurance uh increase  for specifically for that purpose and  two sourcing out the long-term issue of  social care where people lose their  homes uh if they have specific types of  illnesses for example dementia uh which  has always seemed to me to be cruel and  unfair particularly when uh you're you  know helped by the nhs if you had  something else and that was eventually  uh the thing which you passed away from  so uh no we're absolutely committed to  making those changes it's an historic  change that i think will bring a lot of  security to families up and down the the  country so we'll certainly be doing that  okay grant chaps good to talk to you  today thank you for joining us me too  thanks very much

